sections.s2_technical:
GraphQL exposes a single endpoint, typically `/graphql`, and the client sends a query describing the exact shape of data it wants. The server has a strongly typed schema defining types, fields, and the relationships between them, plus queries for reading, mutations for writing, and subscriptions for real-time updates. The response mirrors the query's shape exactly, so the client gets no more and no less than it asked for.

```graphql
query {
  account(number: "SAV2001") {
    balance
    transactions(last: 3) { amount type }
    card { limit }
  }
}
```

This cures two REST problems. Over-fetching happens when a REST endpoint like `GET /accounts/SAV2001` returns twenty fields but the mobile screen needs only the balance, wasting bandwidth and forcing the client to ignore most of the payload. Under-fetching happens when assembling one screen needs the account, then its transactions, then its card, which is three REST round trips. GraphQL collapses both: the client names only balance, three transaction amounts, and the card limit, and receives exactly that in one request.

On the server, each field is backed by a resolver, a function that knows how to fetch that field's data. The account resolver loads the account, the transactions resolver loads transactions for that account, and the card resolver loads the card. GraphQL stitches the results into the requested shape. A critical performance concern is the N+1 problem: if you fetch a list of accounts and then each account's card one by one, you issue one query plus N queries. The standard fix is a DataLoader, which batches and caches the per-item fetches into a single query.

```java
// Spring for GraphQL resolver — schema-first, methods map to fields.
@QueryMapping
public Account account(@Argument String number) {
    return accountService.findByNumber(number);
}

@SchemaMapping
public List<Transaction> transactions(Account account, @Argument int last) {
    return transactionService.lastN(account.getNumber(), last);  // batch via DataLoader
}
```

GraphQL has real strengths. A single request serves many client shapes, so a mobile app and a web dashboard can ask for different field sets from the same graph without the server building bespoke endpoints. The typed schema is self-documenting and enables tooling, autocompletion, and compile-time client checks. Evolving the schema by adding fields does not break existing clients, and deprecating fields is explicit, which reduces the need for versioning.

But GraphQL has costs that make REST the better default in many cases. HTTP caching is harder, because everything is a POST to one endpoint, so you lose the simple URL-based caching REST enjoys and must add application-level caching. Rate limiting and cost control are harder, because a single query can be arbitrarily expensive or deeply nested, so you need query depth limits, complexity analysis, and timeouts to prevent abuse — especially important in banking where a malicious deep query could hammer the database. The N+1 problem requires disciplined batching. And for simple resource CRUD, GraphQL is more machinery than REST needs.

When to choose which: use GraphQL when you have many clients with diverse and changing data needs over a richly connected graph, such as a mobile plus web plus partner ecosystem reading overlapping data, where over-fetching and under-fetching genuinely hurt. Use REST when the API is resource-oriented and simple, when you want straightforward HTTP caching and CDN support, for public APIs where simplicity and ubiquity matter, and for file uploads or streaming where REST and other protocols fit better. Many organizations run both: REST for public and simple internal services, GraphQL as an aggregation layer for complex client-facing views. A senior nuance is that GraphQL moves complexity from the client to the server and to query governance; it is a trade, not a free win, and in a regulated banking context the query-cost controls are not optional.

